  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/1dd593e8b48b13ec512a3e0c823ec0aaac4f5341bd519c7bb9b04648f95f4251.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?",
  "worked_solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/2a68cbeceea9173ab7a53d334d073e9779b048ad8946fd3ff81b09adb79cee98.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph II, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph II shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, III and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
